# Landforms: How Weathering, Erosion, and Deposition Sculpt the Earth

> Mountains are raised from below and lowered from above. This lesson
> organises the geomorphic processes and the signature landforms each
> agent of erosion and deposition leaves behind.

## 1. Endogenic and Exogenic Forces

| Force | Energy source | Pace | Processes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Endogenic | The Earth's internal heat | Slow or sudden | Diastrophism, earthquakes, volcanism |
| Exogenic | Solar energy and gravity | Steady and gradual | Weathering, mass movement, erosion, deposition |

[definition]
**Diastrophism** is slow, large-scale deformation of the crust:
**orogenic** movements build mountains through horizontal compression,
while **epeirogenic** movements lift or sink broad continental areas.
Earthquakes and volcanic eruptions are the sudden counterparts.
[/definition]

## 2. Weathering: Decay Without Transport

**Weathering** is the disintegration and decomposition of rocks where
they lie — nothing is carried away.

| Type | Processes | Favoured setting |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Physical (mechanical) | Frost wedging, thermal expansion, exfoliation | Deserts, high mountains |
| Chemical | Oxidation, carbonation, solution, hydration | Warm, humid tropics |
| Biological | Root pressure, burrowing animals | Vegetated lands |

Oxidation rusts iron-rich minerals; carbonation lets carbon-dioxide-rich
water dissolve limestone; hydration swells minerals and cracks them.

## 3. Mass Movements: Gravity's Work

- **Landslides** — rapid slides of rock and debris down a slope.
- **Mudflows** — saturated soil racing along valleys.
- **Soil creep** — the slowest movement, betrayed by bent tree trunks
  and leaning fence posts.

[warning]
Mass movements need no river or wind — only slope and gravity — which
is why deforested, over-steepened hillsides fail so readily during
heavy rain.
[/warning]

## 4. Rivers: Fluvial Landforms

| Stage | Erosional landforms | Depositional landforms |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Upper course (mountains) | V-shaped valley, gorge, waterfall, potholes | Alluvial fan at the foothills |
| Middle course (plains) | Meanders, river cliffs | Floodplain, ox-bow lake (severed meander) |
| Lower course (delta) | Widened, braided channel | Delta with distributaries, levees |

[example]
The Ganga–Brahmaputra delta (the Sundarbans) is the world's largest
delta; an ox-bow lake is the abandoned loop of an over-widened
meander, left behind after the river cuts across its neck.
[/example]

## 5. Glaciers: Ice-Carved Country

| Erosional landforms | Depositional landforms |
| --- | --- |
| Cirque (bowl-shaped hollow) | Moraines — lateral, medial, terminal |
| Arête (knife-edge ridge) | Drumlins (inverted-spoon hills) |
| U-shaped valley and hanging valley | Eskers and outwash plains |
| Fjord — drowned U-valley, as in Norway | Till sheets of mixed debris |

[info title="Ice versus river"]
A glacier's ice load grinds a U-shaped valley, while a river's water
carves a V-shaped one: the cross-section alone reveals which agent
worked the landscape.
[/info]

## 6. Wind: Aeolian Landforms

| Process | Landform |
| --- | --- |
| Deflation and abrasion | Mushroom (pedestal) rocks, ventifacts |
| Saltation and deposition | Barchans (crescent dunes), seif dunes, sand seas |
| Settling of fine dust | Loess — fertile yellow-brown soils, as in China |

[example]
The crescent barchans of the Thar Desert march downwind, horns trailing,
a few metres each year across the sand sea.
[/example]

## 7. Groundwater: Karst Landforms

Carbon-dioxide-charged rainwater dissolves limestone along joints,
hollowing out a distinctive karst landscape (named for the Karst
plateau of Slovenia).

| On the surface | Underground |
| --- | --- |
| Sinkholes (sinks) and dolines | Caverns and solution channels |
| Disappearing streams | Stalactites (ceiling) and stalagmites (floor) |

India's Borra caves (Andhra Pradesh) and Belum caves show this chemistry
at work.

[tip]
Memory hook: stala**c**tite clings to the **c**eiling; stala**g**mite
grows from the **g**round — and may one day meet its partner in a
pillar.
[/tip]

## 8. Quick Revision

@diagram tree id="geomorphic-agents" layout="tree" aria="Erosion agents and their signature landforms"
  "Exogenic agents" -> Rivers
  "Exogenic agents" -> Glaciers
  "Exogenic agents" -> Wind
  "Exogenic agents" -> Groundwater
  Rivers -> "Meanders, deltas"
  Glaciers -> "U-valleys, moraines"
  Wind -> "Barchans, loess"
  Groundwater -> "Sinkholes, caves"
@/diagram

| Agent | Signature erosional form | Signature deposit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| River | V-valley, meander | Delta, floodplain |
| Glacier | U-valley, cirque | Moraine, drumlin |
| Wind | Mushroom rock | Barchan, loess |
| Groundwater | Sinkhole, cave | Stalactite, stalagmite |
